## DESCRIPTION
## ENDDESCRIPTIOIN
## DBsubject(Probability)
## DBchapter(Continuous distributions)
## DBsection(Application of a normal distribution)
## Institution(The College of Idaho)
## Author(E Cabrera, R Cruz)
## MLT(ExamAdmission)
## Level(2)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('The Basic Practice of Statistics')
## AuthorText1('David Moore')
## EditionText1('5e')
## Section1('3')
## KEYWORDS('statistic', 'normal distribution', 'z score')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',  'PGML.pl',
    'answerHints.pl', 'PGstatisticsmacros.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

$mean_all = random(8.9, 9.9, 0.1);
$sd_all   = random(2.0, 2.6, 0.1);

$mean_med = random(10.1, 10.9, 0.1);
$sd_med   = random(1.4,  1.8,  0.1);

do {
    $cutoff_b1 = random(8,  10);
    $cutoff_b2 = random(11, 12);
    $cutoff_a  = random(11, 13);
} until $cutoff_a != $cutoff_b_2;

$z_a  = ($cutoff_a - $mean_all) / $sd_all;
$z_b1 = ($cutoff_b1 - $mean_med) / $sd_med;
$z_b2 = ($cutoff_b2 - $mean_med) / $sd_med;

$pval_a = normal_prob($z_a,   'inf');
$p_b1   = normal_prob('-inf', $z_b1);
$p_b2   = normal_prob('-inf', $z_b2);
$pval_b = $p_b2 - $p_b1;

Context()->flags->set(tolerance => 0.25, tolType => 'absolute');

$ans_a = Compute(.01 * int(10000 * ($pval_a + .00005)));
$cmp_a = $ans_a->cmp->withPostFilter(AnswerHints(
    sub {
        my ($correct, $student, $anshash) = @_;
        return abs($student - $correct) < .5;
    } => ["Close!  Try using more accuracy."],
    100 - $ans_a =>
        ["The question asks for the percent who scored $cutoff_a or better."]
));

$ans_b = Compute(.01 * int(10000 * ($pval_b + .00005)));
$cmp_b = $ans_b->cmp->withPostFilter(AnswerHints(
    sub {
        my ($correct, $student, $anshash) = @_;
        return abs($student - $correct) < .5;
    } => ["Close!  Try using more accuracy."]
));

BEGIN_PGML
Almost all medical schools in the United States require applicants to take the Medical College Admission Test (MCAT). On one exam, the scores of all applicants on the biological sciences part of the MCAT were approximately Normal with mean [$mean_all] and standard deviation [$sd_all]. For applicants who actually entered medical school, the mean score was [$mean_med] and the standard deviation was [$sd_med].

a) What percent of all applicants had scores higher than [$cutoff_a]?

    ANSWER:  [_]{$cmp_a}%

b) What percent of those who entered medical school had scores between [$cutoff_b1] and [$cutoff_b2]? 

    ANSWER: [_]{$cmp_b}%

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
There are a number of ways to find the answers to these problem.  Here, we will calculate [`z`]-scores and using a standard normal table or software to find the answers. 

Recall that a [`z`]-score for a give data value [`x`] that comes from a normal distribution with mean [`\mu`] and standard deviation [`\sigma`] is 

[``` z = \frac{x-\mu}{\sigma}```]

a) [`` z = \frac{[$cutoff_a]-[$mean_all]}{[$sd_all]} = [@ Round($z_a,3) @] ``]
and then using a table or software:
    [``` P(Z > [@ Round($z_a,3) @]) = [$ans_a/100] ```]
    and converting to percent the answer is [$ans_a].

b)  First calculate the [`z`]-scores for the values [$cutoff_b1] and [$cutoff_b2]:

[``` \begin{aligned}
z_1 & = \frac{[$cutoff_b1]-[$mean_med]}{[$sd_med]} = [$z_b1] \\
z_2 & = \frac{[$cutoff_b2]-[$mean_med]}{[$sd_med]} = [$z_b2]
\end{aligned}
```]

Then we calculate 
[``` \begin{aligned}
p_1 & = P(Z < [$z_b1]) = [$p_b1] \\
p_2 & = P(Z < [$z_b2]) = [$p_b2]
\end{aligned} ```]
and then the fraction that scored between [$cutoff_b1] and [$cutoff_b2] is
[``` p_2 - p_1 = [$p_b2]-[$p_b1] = [$p_b2-$p_b1] ```]

So the percent is [$ans_b]%.
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();       # This should be the last executable line in the problem.